('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Pleural Effusion & Empyema','easy','mcq','Which of the following is the most common cause of a transudative pleural effusion?','["A. Parapneumonic effusion","B. Malignancy","C. Congestive cardiac failure","D. Tuberculosis pleuritis"]','C','Congestive cardiac failure (CCF) is the most common cause of a transudative pleural effusion. Transudates have protein <30 g/L and do not meet Light''s criteria. Parapneumonic effusion and TB are exudates; malignancy can be an exudate.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Pleural Effusion & Empyema','easy','mcq','A pleural fluid protein/serum protein ratio of 0.55 is found on analysis. According to Light''s criteria, how should this effusion be classified?','["A. Transudate, because protein ratio is below 0.6","B. Exudate, because the protein ratio exceeds 0.5","C. Transudate, because the LDH ratio must also be checked first","D. Exudate only if pleural LDH also exceeds two-thirds of the upper normal serum LDH"]','B','Light''s criteria classify an effusion as an exudate if ANY ONE of three criteria is met. A pleural protein/serum protein ratio >0.5 alone is sufficient to classify it as an exudate, regardless of the other parameters.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Pleural Effusion & Empyema','easy','mcq','A 65-year-old woman with an ovarian fibroma is found to have a right-sided pleural effusion and ascites. What eponymous syndrome best describes this triad?','["A. Horner syndrome","B. Meigs syndrome","C. Yellow nail syndrome","D. Dressler syndrome"]','B','Meigs syndrome is the triad of a benign ovarian fibroma, right-sided pleural effusion, and ascites. The effusion is typically a transudate and resolves after removal of the ovarian tumour.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Pleural Effusion & Empyema','medium','mcq','Pleural fluid analysis in a 45-year-old man shows: pH 7.10, glucose 1.8 mmol/L, and turbid fluid. Gram stain reveals Gram-positive cocci. What is the most appropriate next step?','["A. Repeat diagnostic thoracentesis in 48 hours","B. Oral antibiotics and outpatient follow-up","C. Insertion of an intercostal drain plus intravenous antibiotics","D. Pleurodesis with talc slurry"]','C','A pleural fluid pH <7.2, glucose <2.2 mmol/L, turbid fluid, and positive Gram stain fulfil empyema criteria. These require chest drain (intercostal drain) insertion combined with intravenous antibiotics. Antibiotics alone are insufficient; talc pleurodesis is used for recurrent malignant effusions.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Pleural Effusion & Empyema','medium','mcq','A patient with known rheumatoid arthritis develops a pleural effusion. Which biochemical finding in the pleural fluid would be most characteristic of a rheumatoid effusion?','["A. Raised adenosine deaminase (ADA) >40 U/L","B. Triglycerides >1.24 mmol/L","C. Very low pleural fluid glucose (<1.6 mmol/L)","D. Raised pleural fluid amylase"]','C','Rheumatoid pleuritis characteristically produces a very low pleural fluid glucose (sometimes <1.6 mmol/L) due to impaired glucose transport across the pleura. This can mimic empyema biochemically. Raised ADA suggests TB; raised triglycerides suggest chylothorax; raised amylase suggests pancreatitis or oesophageal rupture.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Pleural Effusion & Empyema','medium','mcq','During a therapeutic thoracentesis, which volume limit should generally not be exceeded in a single session to avoid re-expansion pulmonary oedema?','["A. 500 mL","B. 1000 mL","C. 1500 mL","D. 2500 mL"]','C','The maximum recommended volume for a single therapeutic thoracentesis session is 1500 mL (1.5 L). Draining more than this rapidly risks re-expansion pulmonary oedema, a rare but potentially fatal complication characterised by acute hypoxia and pulmonary infiltrates.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Pleural Effusion & Empyema','medium','mcq','A 55-year-old woman with recurrent malignant pleural effusion requires drainage every 2 weeks. She has a good performance status. Which intervention offers the best long-term management?','["A. Repeated therapeutic thoracentesis at each episode","B. Talc pleurodesis or indwelling pleural catheter (IPC)","C. Systemic corticosteroids to reduce effusion formation","D. Prophylactic oral diuretics alone"]','B','For recurrent malignant pleural effusions, definitive management options are talc pleurodesis (best sclerosing agent, ~80-90% success) or insertion of an indwelling pleural catheter (PleurX/IPC), which allows ambulatory home drainage. Repeated thoracentesis is invasive and not cost-effective long term; corticosteroids and diuretics do not address malignant effusions effectively.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Pleural Effusion & Empyema','medium','mcq','A 35-year-old HIV-positive man presents with a lymphocyte-predominant exudative pleural effusion and a pleural ADA of 52 U/L. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Malignant mesothelioma","B. Tuberculous pleuritis","C. Parapneumonic empyema","D. Systemic lupus erythematosus pleuritis"]','B','Tuberculous pleuritis typically produces a lymphocyte-predominant exudative effusion with elevated adenosine deaminase (ADA) >40 U/L. ADA >40 U/L has high sensitivity and specificity for TB pleuritis, especially in endemic areas. Empyema is neutrophil-predominant; SLE typically shows a mixed picture; mesothelioma rarely raises ADA significantly.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Pleural Effusion & Empyema','hard','mcq','A patient with bilateral pleural effusions has the following pleural fluid results: protein 28 g/L, LDH 180 U/L, serum LDH 280 U/L (upper limit of normal 200 U/L), serum protein 60 g/L. Applying Light''s criteria, how should this effusion be classified?','["A. Transudate — none of Light''s criteria are met","B. Exudate — pleural LDH/serum LDH ratio >0.6","C. Exudate — pleural protein/serum protein ratio >0.5","D. Exudate — pleural LDH exceeds two-thirds the upper limit of normal serum LDH"]','D','Checking each criterion: (1) pleural protein/serum protein = 28/60 = 0.47 — not met; (2) pleural LDH/serum LDH = 180/280 = 0.64 — not met (ratio needs >0.6); (3) pleural LDH vs 2/3 × 200 = 133 U/L — pleural LDH 180 > 133 — MET. One criterion is sufficient. The effusion is an exudate by criterion 3 despite the protein suggesting a transudate. This is a classic Light''s criteria trap, common in CCF patients on diuretics.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Pleural Effusion & Empyema','hard','mcq','A trauma patient develops a milky pleural effusion following thoracic duct injury during oesophagectomy. Pleural fluid triglycerides are 2.1 mmol/L. After chest drain insertion, the most appropriate next management step is:','["A. Immediate surgical ligation of the thoracic duct","B. Conservative management with low-fat diet, medium-chain triglycerides, and total parenteral nutrition if needed","C. Talc pleurodesis on day 1","D. Systemic octreotide is absolutely contraindicated in chylothorax"]','B','Post-surgical chylothorax (triglycerides >1.24 mmol/L, milky fluid) is initially managed conservatively: low-fat diet with medium-chain triglycerides (MCTs bypass the thoracic duct) or nil-by-mouth with total parenteral nutrition (TPN) to reduce lymphatic flow, allowing spontaneous healing. Octreotide (somatostatin analogue) may also reduce chyle flow. Surgical thoracic duct ligation is reserved for failure of conservative management after 2 weeks or output >1 L/day.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Pneumothorax','easy','mcq','A 22-year-old tall, thin male smoker presents with sudden onset right-sided pleuritic chest pain and dyspnoea. CXR shows a 3 cm rim of air on the right. He is mildly breathless but haemodynamically stable. What is the most appropriate first-line intervention according to BTS guidelines?','["A. Observation for 4 hours and discharge with follow-up","B. Needle aspiration at the 2nd intercostal space, midclavicular line","C. Emergency intercostal chest drain insertion","D. High-flow oxygen and urgent CT chest"]','B','This is a primary spontaneous pneumothorax (PSP) in a young man with a >2 cm rim of air and symptoms. BTS guidelines recommend aspiration (16-18G cannula at 2nd ICS MCL) as first-line for symptomatic PSP or rim >2 cm. Observation is appropriate only for asymptomatic PSP with <2 cm rim. ICD insertion is reserved for failed aspiration.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Pneumothorax','easy','mcq','A patient presents with acute onset dyspnoea following a stab wound to the right chest. On examination: trachea deviated to the left, absent breath sounds on the right, hyper-resonance on percussion on the right, distended neck veins, and BP 80/50 mmHg. What is the immediate management?','["A. Urgent CXR to confirm tension pneumothorax before treatment","B. Needle decompression at the 2nd intercostal space, midclavicular line on the right","C. Insertion of an intercostal chest drain at the 5th ICS midaxillary line","D. IV fluid resuscitation and call cardiothoracic surgery"]','B','This is a tension pneumothorax — a clinical diagnosis requiring immediate needle decompression without waiting for imaging. A 14-16G cannula is inserted at the 2nd intercostal space, midclavicular line on the affected (right) side. Tracheal deviation is AWAY from the pneumothorax. Delaying for CXR is dangerous; ICD can follow needle decompression.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Pneumothorax','easy','mcq','Spontaneous absorption of a pneumothorax occurs at approximately what rate per day without intervention?','["A. 0.5% per day","B. 1.25% per day","C. 3% per day","D. 5% per day"]','B','Spontaneous resorption of a pneumothorax occurs at approximately 1.25% of the lung volume per day. A 20% pneumothorax would therefore take approximately 16 days to resorb naturally. Administering high-flow oxygen (10 L/min) accelerates resorption approximately fourfold by increasing the oxygen/nitrogen gradient.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Pneumothorax','medium','mcq','A 55-year-old man with severe COPD (FEV1 35% predicted) presents with acute worsening of dyspnoea. CXR shows a 2.5 cm right-sided pneumothorax. He is moderately breathless. What is the most appropriate management according to BTS guidelines for secondary spontaneous pneumothorax?','["A. Observe for 4 hours; discharge if stable","B. Aspiration at 2nd ICS MCL; if successful, admit overnight","C. Immediate insertion of intercostal chest drain","D. Non-invasive ventilation to expand the lung"]','C','Secondary spontaneous pneumothorax (SSP) in COPD with a rim >2 cm and moderate symptoms requires immediate intercostal chest drain (ICD) insertion per BTS guidelines. Unlike PSP, all SSP patients should be admitted. Aspiration may be attempted only for mild/moderate symptoms with <2 cm rim. NIV is contraindicated with undrained pneumothorax.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Pneumothorax','medium','mcq','An intercostal drain inserted for a pneumothorax is noted to be swinging but not bubbling. The repeat CXR shows the lung remains partially collapsed. What does this finding most likely indicate?','["A. The drain is patent and the air leak has resolved","B. The drain is blocked and requires flushing","C. The drain is patent but there is a persistent air leak from the lung","D. The drain is patent with no active air leak; the residual pneumothorax is reabsorbing"]','D','Swinging (movement of fluid in the drain tubing with respiration) indicates the drain is patent and communicating with the pleural space. No bubbling indicates there is NO active air leak from the lung at that moment. Combined with a partially collapsed lung on CXR, this suggests the residual air is reabsorbing. If there were an active leak, bubbling would be present. A blocked drain would show neither swinging nor bubbling.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Pneumothorax','medium','mcq','A 28-year-old woman with a history of recurrent bilateral pneumothoraces is found to have thin-walled lung cysts on HRCT. She is a non-smoker. What is the most likely underlying diagnosis?','["A. Cystic fibrosis","B. Lymphangioleiomyomatosis (LAM)","C. Langerhans cell histiocytosis","D. Primary spontaneous pneumothorax with blebs"]','B','Lymphangioleiomyomatosis (LAM) is a rare condition affecting young women, characterised by smooth muscle proliferation in the lymphatics and airways, producing diffuse thin-walled cysts on HRCT. It causes recurrent pneumothoraces and progressive obstructive lung disease. It may be sporadic or associated with tuberous sclerosis. It is a cause of secondary spontaneous pneumothorax.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Pneumothorax','medium','mcq','A 30-year-old female athlete presents with recurrent right-sided pneumothoraces that occur predictably during menstruation. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Marfan syndrome with apical blebs","B. Catamenial pneumothorax due to thoracic endometriosis","C. Lymphangioleiomyomatosis","D. Primary spontaneous pneumothorax — coincidental timing"]','B','Catamenial pneumothorax is caused by thoracic endometriosis, where endometrial tissue in the pleura or diaphragm causes pneumothorax at the time of menstruation (within 24-72 hours of onset of menses). It is almost always right-sided. Treatment involves hormonal suppression and surgical resection of diaphragmatic endometriosis with pleurodesis.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Pneumothorax','hard','mcq','A pilot presents after his second primary spontaneous pneumothorax, successfully managed with aspiration. He is now asymptomatic with full lung re-expansion on CXR. What is the recommended next management step?','["A. Discharge with safety-net advice and follow-up in 6 weeks","B. VATS (video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery) pleurodesis or bullectomy before return to flying","C. Chemical pleurodesis via ICD as an outpatient","D. High-resolution CT to identify blebs, then await a third episode before intervening"]','B','After a second spontaneous pneumothorax, or in high-risk occupations such as pilots and divers (where a pneumothorax in service would be catastrophic), surgical intervention (VATS bullectomy + pleurodesis) is recommended. Return to flying is contraindicated until definitive pleurodesis has been confirmed. The recurrence risk after a second PSP is ~50%, making prophylactic intervention essential for pilots.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Pneumothorax','hard','mcq','A patient with an intercostal chest drain for a large pneumothorax is connected to an underwater seal. The drain is noted to be continuously bubbling vigorously, even during expiration, and the pneumothorax is not resolving after 5 days. What complication has most likely occurred and what is the next management step?','["A. Drain blockage — flush the drain with saline","B. Persistent bronchopleural fistula — refer for surgical intervention (VATS)","C. Incorrect drain position — remove and reinsert","D. Haemothorax — blood clot obstructing the system"]','B','Continuous vigorous bubbling in both phases of respiration indicates a large, persistent air leak, most likely a bronchopleural fistula (BPF) — an abnormal connection between the bronchial tree and pleural space. A BPF that does not resolve after 5 days of chest drainage requires referral for surgical management, typically VATS with stapling of the fistula and mechanical/chemical pleurodesis.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Pulmonary Embolism','easy','mcq','A 45-year-old woman on the oral contraceptive pill presents with sudden pleuritic chest pain and haemoptysis 10 days after a long-haul flight. Her HR is 110 bpm. Using the Wells PE score, which TWO components in this scenario each contribute the MOST points?','["A. Haemoptysis and tachycardia (HR >100)","B. Immobilisation and active malignancy","C. PE as the most likely diagnosis, and signs/symptoms of DVT","D. Previous DVT/PE and immobilisation"]','C','The Wells PE score gives 3 points each for: (1) signs/symptoms of DVT (leg swelling/tenderness) and (2) PE being the most likely diagnosis. All other individual criteria (HR >100, immobilisation, previous DVT/PE, haemoptysis, malignancy) contribute 1 or 1.5 points each. Understanding the weighting is critical for correct pre-test probability stratification.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Pulmonary Embolism','easy','mcq','A 32-year-old woman with a low pre-test probability for pulmonary embolism has a D-dimer result of 380 ng/mL (<500 ng/mL is the normal threshold). What is the most appropriate next step?','["A. CTPA to confirm or exclude PE","B. No further investigation for PE is required","C. V/Q scan as the preferred next investigation","D. Repeat D-dimer in 48 hours"]','B','A negative D-dimer (<500 ng/mL) in a patient with a LOW pre-test probability (e.g., Wells score ≤4) effectively excludes pulmonary embolism with a negative predictive value of approximately 99%. No further investigation for PE is required. D-dimer is used as a rule-out test, not a rule-in test.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Pulmonary Embolism','easy','mcq','Which ECG finding is the MOST COMMON in acute pulmonary embolism?','["A. S1Q3T3 pattern","B. Right bundle branch block (RBBB)","C. Sinus tachycardia","D. Atrial fibrillation"]','C','Sinus tachycardia is the most common ECG finding in acute PE, occurring in the majority of patients. The S1Q3T3 pattern (S wave in lead I, Q wave and T-wave inversion in lead III) is the classic textbook finding but is actually seen in less than 20% of cases. RBBB and right axis deviation can occur with severe PE causing acute right heart strain.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Pulmonary Embolism','medium','mcq','A 68-year-old man with metastatic colorectal cancer presents with a confirmed first episode of PE. After initial anticoagulation, what is the most appropriate long-term anticoagulation strategy?','["A. Warfarin for 3 months (target INR 2-3)","B. Aspirin 75 mg daily indefinitely","C. LMWH or rivaroxaban/edoxaban indefinitely or until cancer resolves","D. Apixaban for 6 months then stop"]','C','Cancer-associated thrombosis (Trousseau syndrome) is a major indication for indefinite anticoagulation as long as the cancer is active. Current guidelines recommend LMWH (traditionally preferred due to better efficacy vs warfarin in cancer) or direct oral anticoagulants (rivaroxaban or edoxaban — HOKUSAI-VTE Cancer, SELECT-D trials), noting higher GI bleed risk with edoxaban/rivaroxaban in GI malignancies.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Pulmonary Embolism','medium','mcq','A 55-year-old woman presents with massive PE: BP 78/45 mmHg, HR 130 bpm, SpO2 82% on room air. She has no contraindications to thrombolysis. What is the most appropriate immediate treatment?','["A. Unfractionated heparin infusion and urgent CTPA","B. Systemic thrombolysis with alteplase 100 mg IV over 2 hours","C. Surgical embolectomy as first-line treatment","D. Rivaroxaban 15 mg twice daily as per DOAC protocol"]','B','Massive PE with haemodynamic instability (SBP <90 mmHg for >15 minutes) requires systemic thrombolysis as first-line treatment in the absence of absolute contraindications. Alteplase 100 mg IV over 2 hours is standard. Anticoagulation with heparin should be started after (or during) thrombolysis. Surgical embolectomy is reserved for absolute thrombolysis contraindications.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Pulmonary Embolism','medium','mcq','Which investigation is preferred for diagnosing PE in a pregnant woman at 28 weeks gestation with a high pre-test probability and a positive D-dimer?','["A. CTPA — gold standard regardless of pregnancy","B. V/Q scan — lower fetal radiation dose than CTPA","C. Compression ultrasonography of the legs only","D. MRI pulmonary angiography as first-line"]','B','In pregnancy, V/Q scanning is preferred over CTPA when possible because it delivers a lower dose of radiation to the fetus (approximately 0.012 mGy vs 0.1 mGy with CTPA). CTPA delivers higher fetal radiation but is preferred if the V/Q scan is indeterminate or unavailable, as maternal diagnosis accuracy is paramount. Both are considered safe in pregnancy. CTPA also exposes maternal breast tissue to significant radiation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Pulmonary Embolism','medium','mcq','A patient with an unprovoked first PE is commenced on anticoagulation. After 3 months of treatment they are reassessed. Which factor would most strongly favour extending anticoagulation indefinitely?','["A. Provoked PE following an 8-hour flight","B. Proximal DVT found on leg ultrasound at the time of diagnosis","C. Positive antiphospholipid antibody syndrome (triple positive)","D. PE occurring in the context of major orthopaedic surgery"]','C','Antiphospholipid syndrome (APS), especially triple positive (lupus anticoagulant, anti-cardiolipin, and anti-beta2 glycoprotein I antibodies), carries an extremely high recurrence risk and is an indication for indefinite anticoagulation. Provoked PE (long flight, surgery) warrants only 3 months. Unprovoked PE generally warrants ≥6 months with indefinite therapy considered. Proximal DVT with PE does not independently mandate indefinite therapy beyond unprovoked status.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Pulmonary Embolism','hard','mcq','A 60-year-old man presents with confirmed submassive PE: BP 118/76 mmHg, HR 108 bpm, echocardiography shows RV dilatation with McConnell''s sign, and troponin T is elevated at 0.08 μg/L. What does McConnell''s sign on echocardiography describe?','["A. Septal bowing towards the left ventricle in systole","B. Right ventricular free wall akinesia with preserved right ventricular apical contractility","C. Paradoxical septal motion indicating right bundle branch block","D. Dilated right atrium with spontaneous echo contrast"]','B','McConnell''s sign is a specific echocardiographic finding in acute PE: akinesia (or severe hypokinesis) of the right ventricular free wall with preserved or hyperdynamic motion of the RV apex. This pattern occurs because the RV apex is tethered to the LV apex and relatively protected. It has high specificity (~94%) but moderate sensitivity (~77%) for acute PE.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Pulmonary Embolism','hard','mcq','A 50-year-old woman with a confirmed PE and a Wells score of 7 undergoes CTPA showing a large right main pulmonary artery thrombus. She is haemodynamically stable, and echocardiography shows moderate RV dysfunction with BNP of 650 pg/mL. Troponin is also elevated. Renal function is normal. She is commenced on anticoagulation. Which statement BEST describes the management dilemma in submassive PE?','["A. Systemic thrombolysis should be given to all patients with RV dysfunction regardless of haemodynamic status","B. The benefit of thrombolysis in submassive PE is clearly established and outweighs bleeding risk in all patients","C. Thrombolysis may prevent haemodynamic deterioration but carries significant bleeding risk; decision requires individualised risk-benefit assessment","D. Catheter-directed thrombolysis has no role in submassive PE management"]','C','Submassive PE (haemodynamically stable but with RV dysfunction and biomarker elevation) represents a management dilemma. PEITHO trial showed thrombolysis reduced haemodynamic decompensation but increased major bleeding and stroke significantly. Current guidelines recommend anticoagulation alone for most, with escalation (thrombolysis/catheter-directed therapy) reserved for clinical deterioration. The decision requires individualised risk-benefit assessment.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Lung Cancer — Clinical Management','easy','mcq','A 65-year-old male smoker has a central hilar mass on CXR. Bronchoscopy biopsy shows small cells with a high nuclear:cytoplasmic ratio, positive for neuroendocrine markers (synaptophysin, chromogranin A, CD56). What is the most likely histological diagnosis?','["A. Squamous cell carcinoma","B. Adenocarcinoma","C. Small cell lung cancer (SCLC)","D. Large cell neuroendocrine carcinoma"]','C','Small cell lung cancer (SCLC) classically presents as a central hilar mass and is characterised histologically by small cells with scant cytoplasm, salt-and-pepper nuclear chromatin, and positive neuroendocrine markers (synaptophysin, chromogranin A, CD56, NCAM). It is strongly associated with smoking and is highly chemosensitive but aggressive.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Lung Cancer — Clinical Management','easy','mcq','A 58-year-old never-smoker Asian woman presents with a peripheral lung adenocarcinoma. Molecular profiling reveals an EGFR exon 19 deletion. Which targeted therapy is recommended as first-line treatment?','["A. Cisplatin + pemetrexed chemotherapy","B. Pembrolizumab monotherapy","C. Osimertinib (3rd-generation EGFR-TKI)","D. Crizotinib (ALK inhibitor)"]','C','EGFR-mutant NSCLC (exon 19 deletion or exon 21 L858R) should receive osimertinib as first-line therapy based on the FLAURA trial, which demonstrated superior progression-free and overall survival compared with erlotinib or gefitinib. Osimertinib also penetrates the CNS effectively. Crizotinib is for ALK-positive; pembrolizumab is for high PD-L1 without targetable mutations.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Lung Cancer — Clinical Management','easy','mcq','A patient with SCLC limited disease achieves complete response after 4 cycles of cisplatin and etoposide with concurrent thoracic radiotherapy. Which additional intervention has been shown to reduce brain metastases by approximately 50% and improve survival?','["A. Maintenance atezolizumab","B. Prophylactic cranial irradiation (PCI)","C. Adjuvant osimertinib","D. Surveillance MRI brain every 3 months"]','B','Prophylactic cranial irradiation (PCI) is recommended for SCLC limited disease patients who achieve a complete or good partial response to chemoradiotherapy. PCI reduces the incidence of brain metastases by approximately 50% and improves overall survival, as the brain is a common sanctuary site for SCLC. It is given as a short course of whole-brain radiotherapy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Lung Cancer — Clinical Management','medium','mcq','A 70-year-old man with squamous cell lung cancer presents with confusion, polydipsia, polyuria, and constipation. Serum calcium is 3.2 mmol/L with a suppressed PTH of 0.8 pmol/L (normal 1.1-6.9). What is the most likely mechanism of his hypercalcaemia?','["A. Lytic bone metastases releasing calcium directly","B. Ectopic PTH secretion by the tumour cells","C. PTHrP (parathyroid hormone-related protein) secretion","D. Excessive 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D production by the tumour"]','C','Squamous cell carcinoma of the lung is the most common cause of humoral hypercalcaemia of malignancy via secretion of PTHrP (parathyroid hormone-related protein). PTHrP mimics PTH at its receptor, causing hypercalcaemia with suppressed PTH levels. This is distinct from hypercalcaemia due to bone metastases. Ectopic PTH (true PTH) secretion by tumours is extremely rare.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Lung Cancer — Clinical Management','medium','mcq','A 62-year-old woman with SCLC presents with progressive facial and arm oedema, dilated veins over the chest and neck, and headache that worsens on bending forward. What complication has she developed, and what is the immediate management?','["A. Cardiac tamponade — pericardiocentesis","B. Superior vena cava obstruction — high-dose dexamethasone and urgent oncology referral","C. Pancoast tumour — chemoradiation","D. Bilateral arm deep vein thrombosis — anticoagulation"]','B','This presentation — facial plethora/oedema, arm oedema, dilated superficial chest and neck veins, and positional headache — is classic for superior vena cava (SVC) obstruction, most commonly caused by SCLC or NHL. Immediate management includes high-dose dexamethasone (to reduce peri-tumoral oedema), urgent oncology review for chemotherapy/radiotherapy, and consideration of SVC stenting for rapid symptom relief.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Lung Cancer — Clinical Management','medium','mcq','A 55-year-old man with a right apical lung tumour presents with pain radiating down the medial aspect of his right arm into the 4th and 5th fingers, wasting of the intrinsic hand muscles, right-sided ptosis, miosis, and anhidrosis. What is the diagnosis and the most appropriate treatment approach?','["A. Adenocarcinoma with brain metastases — systemic chemotherapy","B. Pancoast (superior sulcus) tumour — concurrent chemoradiotherapy followed by surgical resection","C. SVC obstruction with brachial plexopathy — radiotherapy only","D. Mesothelioma with chest wall invasion — palliative care"]','B','A Pancoast (superior sulcus) tumour invades the brachial plexus (C8, T1, T2 roots) causing medial arm pain and intrinsic hand wasting, and invades the stellate ganglion causing ipsilateral Horner syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis). Treatment of resectable Pancoast tumours involves concurrent chemoradiation (cisplatin-based) followed by surgical resection (en bloc with chest wall), achieving better outcomes than surgery or radiotherapy alone.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Lung Cancer — Clinical Management','medium','mcq','A 48-year-old non-smoking woman with ALK-rearranged stage IV lung adenocarcinoma is about to start systemic therapy. Multiple brain metastases are detected on staging MRI. Which ALK inhibitor has the best CNS penetration and is preferred as first-line treatment?','["A. Crizotinib — first-generation ALK inhibitor with proven CNS activity","B. Alectinib — second-generation ALK inhibitor with superior CNS penetration (ALEX trial)","C. Ceritinib — preferred for CNS disease in treatment-naive patients","D. Lorlatinib — only used after crizotinib failure"]','B','Alectinib is the preferred first-line ALK inhibitor for ALK-rearranged NSCLC, including patients with brain metastases. The ALEX trial demonstrated superior progression-free survival over crizotinib, with dramatically better CNS activity. Crizotinib has poor CNS penetration; lorlatinib (3rd generation) is used after alectinib resistance. ALK-rearranged NSCLC occurs in young non-smokers and adenocarcinoma histology.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Lung Cancer — Clinical Management','hard','mcq','A 72-year-old man with SCLC and hyponatraemia (Na+ 121 mmol/L) undergoes further investigation: serum osmolality 252 mOsm/kg, urine osmolality 480 mOsm/kg, urine sodium 68 mmol/L. He is clinically euvolaemic and not on any causative medications. What paraneoplastic syndrome is occurring, and which further test would confirm it?','["A. Cerebral salt wasting — 24-hour urinary sodium collection","B. SIADH — no further confirmatory test required given the clinical and biochemical picture","C. Addison''s disease — short synacthen test (SST)","D. Hypothyroidism — TFTs"]','B','This biochemical picture is diagnostic of SIADH (Syndrome of Inappropriate Antidiuretic Hormone secretion): hyponatraemia, low serum osmolality, inappropriately elevated urine osmolality (>100 mOsm/kg, here 480), elevated urine sodium (>40 mmol/L in a euvolaemic patient), and clinical euvolaemia with no causative medications. SCLC ectopically secretes ADH. The clinical and biochemical picture here is diagnostic; no additional confirmatory test is required.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Lung Cancer — Clinical Management','hard','mcq','A patient with SCLC develops Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome (LEMS). Which feature best distinguishes LEMS from myasthenia gravis (MG)?','["A. Proximal muscle weakness is seen in both conditions equally","B. In LEMS, muscle strength IMPROVES with repetitive stimulation or exercise, and deep tendon reflexes are reduced","C. Anti-AChR antibodies are positive in LEMS","D. In LEMS, ocular and bulbar involvement is more prominent than limb weakness"]','B','LEMS is caused by auto-antibodies against voltage-gated calcium channels (VGCC) at the pre-synaptic neuromuscular junction, impairing acetylcholine vesicle release. Key distinguishing features from MG: (1) strength IMPROVES with repetitive activity (post-tetanic potentiation) — opposite to MG; (2) deep tendon reflexes are reduced or absent (present in MG); (3) autonomic features (dry mouth, constipation, erectile dysfunction); (4) ocular/bulbar involvement less prominent than in MG.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Respiratory Failure & Mechanical Ventilation','easy','mcq','A patient with an acute COPD exacerbation has the following arterial blood gas on room air: pH 7.30, PaO2 6.8 kPa, PaCO2 8.2 kPa, HCO3 30 mmol/L. Which type of respiratory failure does this represent?','["A. Type 1 respiratory failure — hypoxaemia with normocarbia","B. Type 2 respiratory failure — hypoxaemia with hypercapnia","C. Metabolic alkalosis masking respiratory acidosis","D. Mixed metabolic and respiratory alkalosis"]','B','Type 2 respiratory failure is defined as PaO2 <8 kPa WITH PaCO2 >6 kPa. This patient has both hypoxaemia (PaO2 6.8 kPa) and hypercapnia (PaCO2 8.2 kPa), indicating ventilatory failure (pump failure). The elevated bicarbonate (30 mmol/L) indicates renal compensation for chronic hypercapnia, consistent with acute-on-chronic Type 2 RF in COPD. The pH of 7.30 confirms uncompensated respiratory acidosis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Respiratory Failure & Mechanical Ventilation','easy','mcq','According to the Berlin definition, what PaO2/FiO2 ratio threshold is used to define ARDS?','["A. PaO2/FiO2 <400 mmHg on any supplemental oxygen","B. PaO2/FiO2 <300 mmHg on PEEP ≥5 cmH2O","C. PaO2/FiO2 <200 mmHg on PEEP ≥10 cmH2O","D. PaO2/FiO2 <100 mmHg on any oxygen therapy"]','B','The Berlin definition of ARDS requires: (1) acute onset within 7 days of a known trigger; (2) bilateral infiltrates on CXR not explained by effusions, lobar collapse, or nodules; (3) PaO2/FiO2 ratio <300 mmHg on PEEP ≥5 cmH2O; (4) respiratory failure not fully explained by cardiac failure or fluid overload. Severity: mild P/F 200-300; moderate 100-200; severe <100.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Respiratory Failure & Mechanical Ventilation','easy','mcq','In a patient with an acute COPD exacerbation and a pH of 7.29, PaCO2 of 9 kPa on controlled oxygen therapy, which ventilatory support is the FIRST-LINE intervention if there are no contraindications?','["A. Immediate intubation and invasive mechanical ventilation","B. Non-invasive ventilation (NIV/BiPAP)","C. High-flow nasal oxygen (HFNO)","D. CPAP (continuous positive airway pressure)"]','B','NIV (BiPAP) is the first-line ventilatory support for acute COPD exacerbation with hypercapnic acidosis (pH 7.25-7.35, PaCO2 >6 kPa). It reduces the need for intubation by approximately 50% and reduces mortality. CPAP provides single-pressure support suitable for Type 1 RF (cardiogenic pulmonary oedema, OSA) but does not augment ventilation. HFNO is for Type 1 RF. Intubation is reserved for NIV failure or contraindications to NIV.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Respiratory Failure & Mechanical Ventilation','medium','mcq','A 70 kg (ideal body weight) patient with severe ARDS (P/F ratio 85 mmHg) is intubated and mechanically ventilated. What tidal volume strategy should be used, and why?','["A. 12 ml/kg IBW — ensures adequate CO2 clearance and prevents atelectasis","B. 8 ml/kg IBW — a reasonable compromise between ventilation and lung protection","C. 6 ml/kg IBW — lung-protective ventilation reduces ventilator-induced lung injury and mortality","D. Tidal volume should be guided by plateau pressure alone, not body weight"]','C','Lung-protective ventilation using tidal volumes of 6 ml/kg ideal body weight (IBW) reduces mortality in ARDS compared with conventional 12 ml/kg (ARDSNet trial). Low tidal volumes reduce volutrauma (overdistension injury). Target plateau pressure ≤30 cmH2O. Permissive hypercapnia (allowing PaCO2 to rise) is acceptable. This strategy is the standard of care in ARDS.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Respiratory Failure & Mechanical Ventilation','medium','mcq','A patient with severe ARDS (P/F <150 mmHg) on lung-protective ventilation continues to have refractory hypoxaemia despite optimised PEEP and FiO2 0.8. Which intervention has been shown to improve mortality in this situation?','["A. Inhaled nitric oxide to reduce pulmonary hypertension","B. Prone positioning for at least 16 hours per day","C. Increasing tidal volume to 10 ml/kg IBW to improve alveolar recruitment","D. Early tracheostomy within 48 hours of intubation"]','B','Prone positioning for ≥16 hours per day in severe ARDS (P/F <150 mmHg) reduces 28-day and 90-day mortality (PROSEVA trial, 16% absolute mortality reduction). Prone positioning improves V/Q matching by recruiting dependent lung zones and reducing the dorsal atelectasis that predominates in supine ARDS. Inhaled nitric oxide improves oxygenation transiently but does not reduce mortality.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Respiratory Failure & Mechanical Ventilation','medium','mcq','A patient with Type 1 respiratory failure due to acute cardiogenic pulmonary oedema is in acute distress with SpO2 82% on 15 L/min oxygen via non-rebreather mask. Which respiratory support modality should be initiated next, and why?','["A. NIV/BiPAP — to increase minute ventilation and clear CO2","B. CPAP — to provide continuous positive pressure reducing preload and improving oxygenation without augmenting ventilation","C. HFNO — as the preferred first-line for all Type 1 respiratory failure","D. Intubation — CPAP is contraindicated in cardiogenic pulmonary oedema"]','B','CPAP is the preferred non-invasive support for cardiogenic pulmonary oedema (APO). It provides continuous positive airway pressure which: (1) recruits collapsed alveoli; (2) reduces preload and afterload (beneficial in LV failure); (3) improves oxygenation. BiPAP/NIV also works but is typically used when hypercapnia develops or CPAP fails. CPAP reduces intubation rates and mortality in APO. HFNO can be used but CPAP has stronger evidence in APO.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Respiratory Failure & Mechanical Ventilation','medium','mcq','A 35-year-old morbidly obese woman (BMI 48 kg/m²) with obesity hypoventilation syndrome (OHS) presents with acute-on-chronic hypercapnic respiratory failure. ABG: pH 7.32, PaCO2 9.5 kPa, HCO3 36 mmol/L, PaO2 7.1 kPa. Which feature in her ABG most indicates chronic hypercapnia?','["A. The elevated PaCO2 of 9.5 kPa","B. The elevated bicarbonate of 36 mmol/L indicating chronic renal compensation","C. The low pH of 7.32","D. The reduced PaO2 of 7.1 kPa"]','B','The markedly elevated bicarbonate (36 mmol/L, normal 22-26 mmol/L) is the hallmark of chronic hypercapnia. In chronic Type 2 respiratory failure, the kidneys compensate by retaining bicarbonate (HCO3) to buffer the chronically elevated CO2, partially normalising the pH. The degree of compensation (expected HCO3 rise = 3.5 × Δ PaCO2 in kPa in chronic setting) indicates this has been occurring long-term, consistent with OHS.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Respiratory Failure & Mechanical Ventilation','hard','mcq','A 28-year-old man with severe acute asthma is intubated after failing NIV. During ventilation, the anaesthetist notes progressively rising peak airway pressures and the patient becomes hypotensive. Bilateral breath sounds are present. What is the most likely cause and corrective action?','["A. Tension pneumothorax — needle decompression immediately","B. Dynamic hyperinflation (auto-PEEP) — disconnect from ventilator briefly and apply manual chest compression during expiration","C. Kinked endotracheal tube — replace the ETT","D. Right mainstem intubation — withdraw ETT by 2 cm"]','B','In mechanically ventilated asthma patients, severely obstructed airways cause air trapping (dynamic hyperinflation/auto-PEEP). Air stacks with each breath, causing progressive hyperinflation, rising peak pressures, impaired venous return, and hypotension. The initial management is to disconnect the patient from the ventilator momentarily to allow passive exhalation, followed by prolonged expiratory time (I:E ratio 1:3 or longer) and reduced respiratory rate to prevent recurrence.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Respiratory Failure & Mechanical Ventilation','hard','mcq','A patient with ARDS on lung-protective ventilation has a ROX index calculated at 12 hours of high-flow nasal oxygen (HFNO) therapy prior to intubation. The ROX index is SpO2/FiO2 divided by respiratory rate. SpO2 is 93%, FiO2 is 0.6, and RR is 28 breaths/min. Calculate the ROX index and interpret the result.','["A. ROX index = 5.5 — below threshold, intubation likely not needed","B. ROX index = 5.5 — above threshold, suggests HFNO is succeeding","C. ROX index = 5.5 — but the threshold for predicting intubation need is 4.88; this value suggests HFNO may be succeeding but requires close monitoring","D. ROX index = 3.2 — below the 4.88 threshold, predicting likely need for intubation"]','C','ROX index = (SpO2/FiO2) / RR = (93/0.6) / 28 = 155 / 28 = 5.54. A ROX index <4.88 at 12 hours predicts high likelihood of HFNO failure and need for intubation. A value of 5.54 is above this threshold, suggesting HFNO is currently successful. However, serial measurements and close clinical monitoring are essential, as deterioration can be rapid in patients with Type 1 RF on HFNO.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Respiratory Failure & Mechanical Ventilation','hard','mcq','A patient with ARDS secondary to severe pancreatitis has been prone for 18 hours with improvement in P/F ratio from 88 to 160 mmHg. The team is considering a course of dexamethasone. Which statement regarding pharmacological therapy in ARDS is MOST ACCURATE?','["A. Dexamethasone 6 mg/day for 10 days has been shown to reduce 28-day mortality in moderate-severe ARDS","B. High-dose methylprednisolone (30 mg/kg) given within 3 hours of ARDS onset reduces mortality","C. Corticosteroids are contraindicated in ARDS due to increased risk of ventilator-associated pneumonia","D. Dexamethasone should only be given if the patient also has COVID-19 as the ARDS trigger"]','A','The CoDEX trial demonstrated that dexamethasone 20 mg/day for 5 days then 10 mg/day for 5 days (or equivalent 6 mg/day) in moderate-severe ARDS significantly increased ventilator-free days and reduced 28-day mortality. This benefit extends to ARDS from any cause (not only COVID-19, where the RECOVERY trial also showed benefit). High-dose steroids given very early showed harm in older trials; moderate doses at appropriate timing show benefit.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Pneumothorax','medium','mcq','A 19-year-old male presents with sudden onset left-sided chest pain and mild dyspnoea. CXR shows a 1.5 cm rim of air on the left. He has no significant past medical history and is haemodynamically stable with no breathlessness at rest. According to BTS guidelines for primary spontaneous pneumothorax, what is the most appropriate management?','["A. Immediate needle aspiration at the 2nd ICS midclavicular line","B. Intercostal chest drain insertion","C. Observe for 4 hours; if stable, discharge with safety-net advice and follow-up in 2-4 weeks","D. High-flow oxygen at 10 L/min and admit overnight for observation"]','C','BTS guidelines for PSP state that if the rim of air is <2 cm AND the patient is asymptomatic, observation for 4 hours is appropriate. If the patient remains stable, they can be discharged with safety-net advice and outpatient follow-up within 2-4 weeks. High-flow oxygen accelerates resorption but does not change the management pathway for small asymptomatic PSP.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Pulmonary Embolism','medium','mcq','A 40-year-old woman is newly diagnosed with unprovoked proximal DVT and PE. She has no contraindication to anticoagulation. Which DOAC regimen is approved for acute treatment and what is the minimum treatment duration recommended?','["A. Apixaban 5 mg twice daily from day 1 for 3 months","B. Rivaroxaban 15 mg twice daily for 21 days, then 20 mg once daily; minimum 3 months (6 months for unprovoked)","C. Dabigatran 150 mg twice daily from day 1 without a lead-in dose for 3 months","D. Warfarin starting on day 1 with LMWH bridging until INR 2-3 for 3 months"]','B','For PE/proximal DVT, rivaroxaban is given as 15 mg twice daily with food for 21 days (acute phase), then 20 mg once daily. Apixaban requires a 10 mg twice daily lead-in for 7 days (not 5 mg). Dabigatran requires initial LMWH for 5-10 days before switching. For an unprovoked PE, minimum treatment is 6 months with indefinite therapy considered due to high recurrence risk (~30% at 5 years).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Clinicals','Lung Cancer — Clinical Management','medium','mcq','A 63-year-old man with stage IIIA NSCLC (squamous cell carcinoma, N2 disease confirmed on EBUS-FNAB) is not suitable for surgical resection. PD-L1 is 35%. EGFR, ALK, ROS1 are wild-type. What is the recommended treatment approach?','["A. Pembrolizumab monotherapy given the PD-L1 >1%","B. Concurrent platinum-based chemoradiation followed by durvalumab consolidation immunotherapy","C. Cisplatin + etoposide chemotherapy alone (as for SCLC)","D. Best supportive care — stage III NSCLC is not amenable to curative-intent treatment"]','B','Unresectable stage III NSCLC is treated with concurrent platinum-based chemoradiotherapy (e.g., cisplatin + vinorelbine or paclitaxel + carboplatin with concurrent radiotherapy). Patients who do not progress after chemoradiation receive durvalumab (anti-PD-L1) consolidation for up to 12 months (PACIFIC trial), which significantly improves progression-free and overall survival regardless of PD-L1 status. Pembrolizumab monotherapy is for stage IV with PD-L1 ≥50%.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');